We then fit the SSF model.
This is a step-selection function fitted with conditional logistic regression. It compares each observed step (case_ = 1) with its available/random alternatives (case_ = 0) within the same step_id_ stratum.
```{r}
library(survival)

m <- ssf_dat |>
  mutate(log_sl_ = log(sl_), cos_ta_ = cos(ta_),
         tailwind_s = scale(tailwind)[, 1],
         cross_s    = scale(abs_cross)[, 1]) |>
  fit_clogit(case_ ~ tailwind_s + cross_s +
               sl_ + log_sl_ + cos_ta_ + strata(step_id_))
summary(m)
```
What each term does

sl_ (step length). The linear step-length term. Its coefficient adjusts the rate parameter of the gamma distribution. A negative coefficient means shorter steps are favored relative to what the random steps assumed, and a positive one means longer steps are.

log_sl_ (log of step length). Its coefficient adjusts the shape parameter of the gamma distribution. Together with sl_, it lets the model correct the whole step-length distribution rather than only its mean. Both are needed because the gamma density involves both sl_ and log(sl_).

cos_ta_ (cosine of the turning angle). This adjusts the concentration of the von Mises distribution, meaning how strongly the bird keeps going straight. A positive coefficient means straighter movement than assumed, and a negative one means more turning or reversals. The cosine is used because it peaks at 0 (straight ahead) and is lowest at π (reversal), and because the von Mises density is built from it.



Trying to slim the model down 

```{r}
library(amt); library(dplyr); library(survival)

dat <- ssf_dat |>
  mutate(log_sl_    = log(sl_),
         cos_ta_    = cos(ta_),
         tailwind_s = as.numeric(scale(tailwind)),
         cross_s    = as.numeric(scale(abs_cross)))

# slim model: wind covariates only
m_slim <- fit_clogit(dat, case_ ~ tailwind_s + cross_s + strata(step_id_))
summary(m_slim)

# full model (movement terms included), for comparison
m_full <- fit_clogit(dat, case_ ~ tailwind_s + cross_s +
                       sl_ + log_sl_ + cos_ta_ + strata(step_id_))

summary(m_full)
```
The wind coefficients are essentially identical in both models, so the movement terms (sl_, log_sl_, cos_ta_) aren’t driving anything. The full model’s likelihood ratio test is only 0.06 higher than the slim one’s, for 3 extra parameters (p ≈ 0.99), so those terms add nothing. Use the slim model.


